{
  "title": "Hash tables",
  "subtitle": "Turning a key into an address",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "type": "table",
      "chapter": "The problem of scale",
      "title": "Finding one number among 10 million",
      "narration": "Say you keep ten million phone numbers, and you need to find one of them, again and again, thousands of times a second.",
      "columns": [
        "How it is stored",
        "Checks for 10 million, at most",
        "Grows like"
      ],
      "rows": [
        {
          "cells": [
            "A list in any order",
            "10,000,000",
            "O(n)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "cells": [
            "A sorted list, with binary search",
            "24",
            "O(log n)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "cells": [
            "A hash table",
            "about 1",
            "O(1)"
          ],
          "hidden": true
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": 0,
          "say": "In a list with no order, you may have to check every one of the ten million."
        },
        {
          "focus": 1,
          "say": "Sort the list and you can halve it on each check, so twenty four checks are enough."
        },
        {
          "focus": "Checks for 10 million, at most@1",
          "say": "That is fast. But when a busy system does this thousands of times a second, even twenty four checks add up."
        },
        {
          "show": [
            2
          ],
          "tint": {
            "2": "good"
          },
          "say": "What we want is one jump, straight to the spot where the number lives. No scanning, no halving. That is what a hash table gives you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "array",
      "chapter": "The hash function",
      "title": "Working out where \"cat\" goes",
      "narration": "The trick is to work out the spot from the key itself. The rule that does this is called a hash function.",
      "arrays": [
        {
          "name": "key",
          "label": "\"cat\"",
          "values": [
            "c",
            "a",
            "t"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "table",
          "label": "table",
          "values": [
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ]
        }
      ],
      "pointers": [
        {
          "name": "h(cat)",
          "array": "table",
          "at": 0,
          "hidden": true
        }
      ],
      "callout": "h(key) = (sum of character codes) mod 8",
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": "array:key",
          "say": "Our key is the word cat."
        },
        {
          "set": {
            "key[0]": 99,
            "key[1]": 97,
            "key[2]": 116
          },
          "callout": "c = 99,  a = 97,  t = 116",
          "say": "Every letter is stored as a number. C is 99, a is 97, and t is 116."
        },
        {
          "focus": "array:key",
          "callout": "99 + 97 + 116 = 312",
          "say": "Add them up, and you get 312."
        },
        {
          "focus": "array:table",
          "callout": "312 mod 8 = 0",
          "say": "The table has only eight slots, so we keep the remainder after dividing by eight. 312 divided by eight leaves 0."
        },
        {
          "show": [
            "ptr:h(cat)"
          ],
          "set": {
            "table[0]": "cat"
          },
          "tint": {
            "table[0]": "good"
          },
          "say": "So cat goes in slot zero."
        },
        {
          "focus": [
            "ptr:h(cat)",
            "table[0]"
          ],
          "callout": "h(\"cat\") = 0",
          "say": "To find cat later, we do not search. We do the same sum, get zero again, and read slot zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "array",
      "chapter": "A collision",
      "title": "\"act\" lands on the same slot",
      "narration": "Now add a second word, act.",
      "arrays": [
        {
          "name": "key",
          "label": "\"act\"",
          "values": [
            "a",
            "c",
            "t"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "table",
          "label": "table",
          "values": [
            "cat",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ]
        }
      ],
      "pointers": [
        {
          "name": "h(act)",
          "array": "table",
          "at": 0,
          "hidden": true
        }
      ],
      "tint": {
        "table[0]": "good"
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "set": {
            "key[0]": 97,
            "key[1]": 99,
            "key[2]": 116
          },
          "callout": "97 + 99 + 116 = 312",
          "say": "It has the same letters as the first word, in a different order, so its sum is also 312."
        },
        {
          "show": [
            "ptr:h(act)"
          ],
          "callout": "312 mod 8 = 0",
          "say": "And the remainder is zero again, so act points at slot zero."
        },
        {
          "tint": {
            "table[0]": "bad"
          },
          "callout": "[collision]{bad}",
          "say": "But cat is already there. Two different keys want the same slot. This is called a collision."
        },
        {
          "focus": "array:table",
          "callout": "endless words, 8 slots",
          "say": "You cannot avoid it. There are endless possible words, and only eight slots. Put ten pigeons in nine holes, and some hole gets two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "memory",
      "chapter": "Fix one: chaining",
      "title": "Each slot holds a short list",
      "narration": "The first fix is called chaining. Each slot holds the start of a small linked list instead of one key.",
      "frames": [],
      "objects": [
        {
          "id": "slots",
          "type": "table, 8 slots",
          "fields": {
            "[0]": {
              "ref": "n1"
            },
            "[1]": "null",
            "[2]": "null",
            "[3] to [7]": "null"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "n1",
          "type": "node",
          "fields": {
            "key": "\"cat\"",
            "next": "null"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "n2",
          "type": "node",
          "fields": {
            "key": "\"act\"",
            "next": "null"
          },
          "hidden": true
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": [
            "slots.[0]",
            "n1"
          ],
          "say": "Slot zero points at a node holding cat."
        },
        {
          "show": [
            "n2"
          ],
          "set": {
            "n1.next": {
              "ref": "n2"
            }
          },
          "callout": "slot 0: \"cat\" → \"act\" → null",
          "say": "When act collides with it, act just joins the end of that list."
        },
        {
          "focus": [
            "slots.[0]",
            "n1",
            "n1.next",
            "n2"
          ],
          "callout": "find \"act\": slot 0, then 2 checks",
          "say": "To find act, jump to slot zero, then walk the list until you meet act. Here that is two checks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "array",
      "chapter": "Fix two: open addressing",
      "title": "Take the next free slot",
      "narration": "The second fix is called open addressing. Every key lives in the table itself, and a taken slot sends you looking further along.",
      "values": [
        "cat",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        ""
      ],
      "pointers": [
        {
          "name": "probe",
          "at": 0
        }
      ],
      "tint": {
        "0": "good"
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": [
            "ptr:probe",
            0
          ],
          "callout": "h(\"act\") = 0",
          "say": "Act starts at slot zero, its hash."
        },
        {
          "tint": {
            "0": "bad"
          },
          "callout": "slot 0 is taken",
          "say": "Slot zero is taken by cat."
        },
        {
          "move": {
            "probe": 1
          },
          "set": {
            "1": "act"
          },
          "tint": {
            "0": "good",
            "1": "good"
          },
          "callout": "try slot 1: free",
          "say": "So it steps to the next slot, slot one, which is free, and act goes there."
        },
        {
          "focus": [
            0,
            1
          ],
          "callout": "find \"act\": slot 0, then slot 1",
          "say": "To find act later, start at slot zero, see cat, and step along until you meet act or an empty slot."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "array",
      "chapter": "Load factor",
      "title": "When the table gets crowded",
      "narration": "With chaining, the danger is a crowded table. Here seven keys sit in eight slots, and each list hangs down below its slot.",
      "arrays": [
        {
          "name": "old0",
          "label": "8 slots",
          "values": [
            "cat",
            "",
            "owl",
            "ant",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "old1",
          "label": "",
          "values": [
            "act",
            "",
            "dog",
            "hen",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "old2",
          "label": "",
          "values": [
            "pig",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "new0",
          "label": "16 slots",
          "values": [
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ],
          "hidden": true
        },
        {
          "name": "new1",
          "label": "",
          "values": [
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            "",
            ""
          ],
          "hidden": true
        }
      ],
      "callout": "load factor α = N / M",
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": [
            "array:old0",
            "array:old1",
            "array:old2"
          ],
          "callout": "α = 7 / 8 = 0.875",
          "say": "The load factor is the number of keys divided by the number of slots. Seven over eight is zero point eight seven five."
        },
        {
          "tint": {
            "old0[0]": "bad",
            "old1[0]": "bad",
            "old2[0]": "bad",
            "old0[2]": "bad",
            "old1[2]": "bad",
            "old0[3]": "bad",
            "old1[3]": "bad"
          },
          "say": "The keys do not spread out evenly, so the lists grow. Slot zero holds 3 keys, and finding pig means walking past cat and act first."
        },
        {
          "show": [
            "array:new0",
            "array:new1"
          ],
          "tint": {
            "array:old0": "dim",
            "array:old1": "dim",
            "array:old2": "dim"
          },
          "focus": [
            "array:new0",
            "array:new1"
          ],
          "say": "So the table grows. We make a new one with twice the slots, sixteen."
        },
        {
          "set": {
            "new0[0]": "pig",
            "new0[2]": "owl",
            "new0[3]": "ant",
            "new0[8]": "cat",
            "new1[8]": "act",
            "new0[10]": "dog",
            "new0[11]": "hen"
          },
          "tint": {
            "new0[0]": "good",
            "new0[2]": "good",
            "new0[3]": "good",
            "new0[8]": "bad",
            "new1[8]": "bad",
            "new0[10]": "good",
            "new0[11]": "good"
          },
          "callout": "α = 7 / 16 ≈ 0.44",
          "say": "Then every key is hashed again, this time with the remainder after dividing by sixteen, and moves to its new slot. Most lists shrink to one key."
        },
        {
          "focus": [
            "new0[8]",
            "new1[8]"
          ],
          "callout": "312 mod 16 = 8",
          "say": "Cat and act still share slot eight, because their sums are equal whatever the size. Real hash functions mix in the order of the letters, so this happens far less."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "chapter": "The guarantee",
      "title": "What a hash table costs",
      "narration": "Many tables grow once the load factor passes about two thirds to three quarters. Then the lists stay short, and every operation is one jump plus a check or two.",
      "columns": [
        "Operation",
        "Average",
        "Worst case"
      ],
      "rows": [
        {
          "cells": [
            "Look up",
            "O(1)",
            "O(n)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "cells": [
            "Insert",
            "O(1)",
            "O(n)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "cells": [
            "Delete",
            "O(1)",
            "O(n)"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "focus": "col:Average",
          "tint": {
            "Average@0": "good",
            "Average@1": "good",
            "Average@2": "good"
          },
          "say": "On average, looking up, adding and removing all take the same short time, however big the table gets."
        },
        {
          "focus": "col:Worst case",
          "say": "The worst case is every key in one slot, which a good hash function makes very unlikely."
        },
        {
          "focus": "Average@1",
          "say": "Growing the table is slow when it happens, but it happens so rarely that it adds only a little to each insert on average. That is how dictionaries, database indexes and caches answer in an instant."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
